---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="About — Writings" description="A short note on who this site is for.">
  <article class="container-narrow about">
    <p class="hand kicker">about</p>
    <h1>Hi, I'm Lala.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      This is a quiet place on the internet where I keep what I'm learning —
      about content systems, faith, health, ai, and the long and imperfect
      obedience of building a life that keeps growing in love with God and
      His people.
    </p>
    <p>
      I work in content marketing for a B2B SaaS company by day, helping others
      learn freelancing by night, and try to walk faithfully with God through it all.
      Some of what's here is professional. Some of it is devotional. Some of it
      is field notes from a life in progress.
    </p>
    <p>
      Nothing here is polished. It's a journal, not a portfolio. But it's a
      journal that wants to be useful — so if you find something that helps,
      that's the whole point.
    </p>

    <section class="faq" aria-labelledby="about-faq-heading">
      <p class="hand faq-kicker">who is lala?</p>
      <h2 id="about-faq-heading">Frequently asked questions</h2>

      <details>
        <summary>How did an ordinary girl from a humble corner of Taguig end up with thousands of people listening?</summary>
        <p>
          Honestly, I still ask the same question. I am not glamorous. I do not
          have a fancy office. I did not come from a famous family. For a long
          time the voice in my head said, "Who are you to even speak here?" Then
          I learned something simple. Information is free. Anyone can Google a
          tutorial. What people are actually starving for is someone real.
          Someone who tells the truth and answers messages like there is a tired
          human on the other side of the screen. That is the whole thing.
          Genuine content and kindness. No secret recipe.
        </p>
        <p>
          I am not clever enough to have built this on my own, and I stopped
          pretending I was. The words, the story, the small audience, the
          strength to show up on sick days, all of it was given to me by God. I
          just try to take care of it honestly instead of performing it.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>What was your family like growing up?</summary>
        <p>
          We were a family of eight. Two girls and five boys. We were very poor
          back then. My mama was a street vendor. She sold cooked food so we
          could eat. My papa was a security guard. He guarded a school so we
          could go to school. By 2010, no one in our family had graduated yet. I
          was a government scholar studying Computer Engineering. I tutored
          Korean students for five thousand pesos a month. I was a student
          assistant for three thousand more. The government paid my tuition. I
          worked for everything else.
        </p>
        <p>
          Not every family on our street made it out. I still think about the
          ones just as poor as us whose children never got to finish. I do not
          know why mine did and theirs did not, and I have stopped trying to
          sound like I do. What I know is what happened in my house. I graduated
          in 2011. Then my brother. Then my sister. Now we are helping the
          youngest finish. For years I was sure God had forgotten us. He had not.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>You call yourself a rare-disease survivor. What does that actually mean for you?</summary>
        <p>
          It is scary to say out loud, but yes. I was born with a blood
          condition called hereditary spherocytosis. My skin was yellow for
          years. I no longer have a spleen or a gall bladder. My spine is weak.
          My brain is prone to bleed. I get sick easily. This is not a story
          about getting better. I am not fixed. The brain still bleeds. I still
          get sick. I am not writing this from the safe side of it. I am writing
          from inside it.
        </p>
        <p>
          There are days the fear is loud. "You will always be the sick one.
          Your body will fail you again." I will not pretend the pain is small.
          But the sickness does not get to say who I am. A long time ago a man
          named Jesus let Himself be broken on purpose, for people who could not
          save themselves. I am one of those people. That is why I can say I am
          held even on the days my body is not holding. There is a kind of joy in
          me that the pain cannot reach. I did not manufacture it, so I cannot
          lose it.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>You write about God like He is everything. Were you always this way?</summary>
        <p>
          No. Not even close. For the first twenty years of my life I was bitter
          and angry with God. The voice in my head asked, "Did God make a
          mistake when He created me? Am I just a mistake?" I grew up poor,
          yellow, and weak, and I blamed Him for all of it. People assume a
          faith like mine is just what desperate people reach for. And I was
          desperate, I will give them that. But desperation did not invent the
          God who met me in it. I did not climb my way back up to God. I was too
          angry and too tired to climb. He came down into the dark and found me
          there. My grip on Him kept slipping for twenty years. His grip on me
          never once did.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>Who is AJ?</summary>
        <p>
          AJ is my husband. He loved me when I was sick and yellow. He was
          already with me before the surgery, when my skin was yellow, when my
          eyes were yellow, when my body was the thing I was most ashamed of.
          For years I kept a private list of reasons no one would ever stay. He
          never asked to see it. He just stayed. A man chose me when I had
          nothing to offer and every reason to walk away. I had heard my whole
          life about a love you do not have to earn, and I always thought it was
          a nice idea meant for other people. AJ is the one who made me believe
          it was real. He stayed, and somewhere in his staying I finally started
          to see that Someone had been staying with me all along.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>What do you actually do for work?</summary>
        <p>
          What I do now is content marketing. I help a US-based team tell their
          story online. The writing, the social media, and the behind-the-scenes
          work that keeps it all organized. I did not start there. My first real
          job was in a call center doing technical support, including for
          Google. In March 2015 my health made me give it up. So I started over
          from nothing and began freelancing, and my first gig online was email
          support. My body could not keep up with heavier work, so I taught
          myself digital marketing, because it was something I could still do
          sitting down on a bad day. I got certified as a Facebook Blueprint
          Creative Strategist and a Google Educator. One skill, one client, one
          certificate at a time.
        </p>
        <p>
          These days my hours are flexible, which is its own kind of mercy,
          because my body does not always let me work on a fixed schedule. I have
          done this for years and I still wake up afraid. Will today be too much?
          Will my body hold up? So before I start, before I open my emails, I
          give the day away. "Lord, you take care of today." That is the whole
          prayer. Then peace I cannot explain shows up. I am not telling you to
          hustle harder. I am telling you to put it down. I do my work well
          because I care about the person on the other end, not because I am
          still trying to prove I am enough.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>What is this community you built?</summary>
        <p>
          I am the founder of a community of thousands of Filipino freelancers.
          It started in one of the hardest seasons of my own life. I could not
          see myself clearly yet, but I could see other tired people who needed
          someone one step ahead of them. So the pattern became simple. Follow
          someone ahead of you, and help someone behind you. I teach people how
          to start working from home, and I walk with them as they grow. I
          learned something I did not expect along the way. Joy you keep to
          yourself quietly dies. Joy you give away grows. I am not a guru on a
          stage. I am just a little further down the same road, turning around to
          say, keep coming.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>Why do you give so much of it away for free?</summary>
        <p>
          People ask me this with suspicion, like there has to be a catch. I
          understand it. The online world is full of people trying to sell you
          something. So here is the plain answer. It was given to me for free
          first. The strength, the second chance, the people who taught me when I
          had nothing and could not pay them back. I did not earn the seeds, so I
          am not going to hoard the harvest. The day you plant the seed is not
          the day you eat the fruit, and somebody watered mine long before I
          could ever return the favor. This is just me passing the bucket down
          the line.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>How do you keep going when your body keeps failing you?</summary>
        <p>
          Slowly. And not on my own strength. There was a season I was broke,
          broken, and lost. No money, no work, a tumor in my brain, a spine that
          felt sixty years old. The voice said, "Everyone is moving forward. You
          are behind. You will never catch up." I did not get up all at once. I
          got up one small thing at a time. Open the laptop. Send the message.
          Drink the water. Say the prayer, even if it is only one sentence long.
          I am not late. I am being formed. The same hands that hold me now were
          holding me when I had nothing to show for a day except getting through
          it. Patience is not weakness. It is trusting there will still be a hand
          there tomorrow.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>Why did you start writing all of this down?</summary>
        <p>
          Because my time here is not guaranteed, and I have made my peace with
          that. I live in a body that keeps reminding me how fragile life is.
          After one hard hospital season, I spent a whole day with my siblings
          and my nieces, and something quiet settled in me. I do not want to
          waste the life I was given. I want to leave true words for the people I
          love while I still have the time to write them. Not just the good
          parts. The real thing. The bitterness that healed. The fear that got
          smaller. The One who stayed when everyone, including me, had reasons to
          leave. If these words outlast me, I do not want them pointing back at
          me. That is the whole reason this page exists.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>You always say it is not about perfection, it is about direction. What do you mean?</summary>
        <p>
          I mean I gave up trying to be flawless a long time ago. I will never be
          the perfect freelancer, the perfect wife, or the perfect Christian. My
          faith itself rises and falls. Some mornings I believe easily. Some
          mornings I do not. But where I stand with God does not swing with my
          mood, because it was never built on my performance in the first place.
          So I stopped measuring myself by perfection and started measuring by
          direction. Am I still facing the right way? Am I taking the next honest
          step? That is enough for me now. Not perfection. Direction.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>If I only remember one thing about you, what should it be?</summary>
        <p>
          Not me, honestly. If you walk away remembering Lala, I have missed the
          whole point. Here is the truth I would actually want you to keep. I was
          poor. I was sick. I was bitter. I was lost. And somehow I was carried
          the whole way, by a love I did not earn and could not lose. I am not
          anyone special. I am just a person who was held when she had nothing
          left to hold on to. We are each a small dot in a vast universe, and I
          have learned that is not a thing to fear. It means I was never the one
          holding it all together. So if you take one thing from this page, take
          this. You are not too broken, too far behind, or too ordinary to be
          held. I know, because I was all three.
        </p>
      </details>
    </section>

    <p class="glory">For the glory of God.</p>
    <p class="sig hand">— Lala</p>
  </article>
</Base>
<style>
  .about { padding: 3rem 0 5rem; }
